        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not run six dehumidifiers through a winter in a test garage, and we will not pretend otherwise. This guide is built from manufacturer specification sheets, ENERGY STAR records and retailer listings that repeat the manufacturer's figures. Every capacity, temperature and electrical number on this page is attributed to its source, and where listings disagree, which they often do with this kind of equipment, we say so and plan around the more conservative figure.
          </p>

          <p>
            A garage is a harder job than a basement, and that shaped every pick. Garages are colder, dustier and dirtier, they swing in temperature and humidity every time the door opens, and many have no floor drain. So the first thing we looked for was a published minimum operating temperature, because a household dehumidifier that stops working at 41°F is useless in a garage that sits at 38°F all winter. Units that publish a low-temperature floor ranked above those that do not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Second, we looked at drainage. A garage dehumidifier that needs its bucket emptied twice a day will not get emptied twice a day. Built-in pumps or a gravity drain to a floor drain or the driveway are close to essential, and four of our six picks have a pump. Third, we looked for rugged construction, because a garage dehumidifier will get bumped, covered in sawdust and splashed with salt water off the cars.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your problem is a basement rather than a garage, our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">basement dehumidifier guide</a> is the better starting point, and if the whole house is humid, our <a href="/best-whole-house-dehumidifier/">whole house dehumidifier guide</a> covers ducted systems.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="why-garages" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Why Garages Need a Different Dehumidifier
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most household dehumidifiers are refrigerant units: a fan pulls air across a cold coil, water condenses on the coil and drips into a bucket. That works well at room temperature. As the air cools, the coil gets colder, and below a certain point the condensate freezes on the coil instead of dripping off. The unit then has to stop and defrost, and it spends more and more of its time defrosting as the temperature falls. Many household units list a floor around 41°F, and below roughly 50°F their real capacity drops sharply.
          </p>

          <p>
            Low-grain refrigerant, or LGR, units are refrigerant designs engineered to keep working at lower temperatures and humidity. The AlorAir Sentinel and Storm and the Dri-Eaz Revolution are this kind, and their listings give operating floors around 33 to 34°F. They still use a cold coil, so they still slow down near freezing, but they keep pulling water long after a household unit has given up.
          </p>

          <p>
            Desiccant dehumidifiers work differently. They pass air over an absorbent material, usually a rotating wheel, which soaks up moisture, then heat the material to drive the water off into a collection tank. Because nothing gets cold, nothing freezes, and listings for the Ebac DD200 give a minimum of minus 4°F. The price is electricity: a desiccant unit uses a heater, so it draws far more power than a refrigerant unit for the same water removed in warm conditions.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the right type depends on how cold your garage gets. Above about 50°F year-round, a household unit is fine. Between freezing and 50°F, an LGR unit is the sweet spot. If the garage regularly drops below freezing, a desiccant unit is the only one that keeps removing water reliably.
          </p>
        </div>

