        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use a regular dehumidifier in a garage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if the garage stays above roughly 50°F, as many attached garages do. Below that, a household refrigerant unit loses capacity quickly as its coil frosts, and many list a floor around 41°F. For colder garages, choose a low-grain refrigerant unit rated to about 33°F or a desiccant unit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best dehumidifier for a cold garage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a garage that gets cold but rarely freezes, a low-grain refrigerant unit like the AlorAir Sentinel HDi65S, rated down to 33.8°F by AlorAir, is the best balance. For a garage that regularly drops below freezing, a desiccant like the Ebac DD200, listed down to -4°F, is the only type that keeps working.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size dehumidifier do I need for a two-car garage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A typical damp two-car garage suits a 50 to 55 pint unit, such as the AlorAir HDi65S. Go larger, 80 to 85 pints, for a three-car garage, a detached shop or a garage that floods with meltwater from cars. Size up in cold climates, because real capacity falls well below the 80°F rating.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do desiccant dehumidifiers work in freezing temperatures?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. A desiccant dehumidifier absorbs moisture into a material and then heats it off, so nothing gets cold enough to freeze. Ebac lists the DD200 down to -4°F. The trade-off is power: a desiccant unit draws far more electricity than a refrigerant unit in mild weather.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where should I put a dehumidifier in my garage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Put it where air circulates freely, a few inches or more from walls, away from where cars park and off the floor if snowmelt pools there. Place it near a floor drain or where a pump hose can run outside, and keep the garage door closed while it runs.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I drain a dehumidifier in a garage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use a gravity hose to a floor drain if you have one. If not, buy a unit with a built-in pump that can push water to a sink, through a wall or onto the driveway. Keep the line short and sloped, and protect it from freezing in winter.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What humidity should a garage be?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Aim for about 50 percent relative humidity. Above roughly 60 percent, tools rust, mildew grows and stored items start to smell. Going much below 50 percent costs electricity for little benefit. A hygrometer in the garage tells you whether the dehumidifier is keeping up.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
